organic chemistry is based on carbon  containing molecules with CH bonds so  some joke that organic chemists see the  entire periodic table as carbon except  for a few other key elements now the  stereotype kind of makes sense  organic chemistry arose as a discipline  to describe the chemistry of life and  the elements contained within the human  body are concentrated in this upper  right portion of the periodic table but  if we're talking about biochemistry  there are other elements that are  important as well trace elements in the  human body include the transition metals  and many of the reactants that you'll  meet in organic chemistry 1 &amp; 2 span the  entire periodic table all that being  said I'm going to give into stereotypes  for a moment and we're going to focus on  the bonding of elements in the upper  right-hand portion of the periodic table  because these are the most likely  elements to be bonded to carbon in  organic molecules let's briefly review  the octet rule most elements like to  have eight electrons around them  this includes all of the electrons that  are shared in bonds there are many  exceptions to this of course and a  notable one is hydrogen which only makes  one bond and therefore has two electrons  around itself if you're not allowed to  use a periodic table to complete your  exam it's wise to memorize the periodic  table going over from carbon to fluorine  and down the halogens you can use my  silly acronym can normal owls fly or  come up with your own going from left to  right the number of valence electrons  that the element prefers to have  increases and now we get to our little  trick starting with carbon we can count  down from four and we get the number of  bonds that the element likes to form  when it is neutral  now phosphorus and sulfur also follow  this rule but they're special cases  because they have empty D orbitals they  can actually exceed an octet of  electrons around them so phosphorus when  neutral will form either three  we're five bonds and sulfur can form two  four or six bonds to other atoms when  neutral let's look at a simple example  of a molecule containing an element from  each group so we can correlate the  number of valence electrons with a  number of bonds that the element likes  to make here I've drawn out methane with  a pair of dots to represent the two  electrons that are shared in the bond to  hydrogen in organic chemistry it's more  conventional to show the pair of  electrons as a single line connecting  the two atoms keep in mind that each of  these lines is representing a pair of  electrons now to count valence electrons  we can think about splitting the bond in  half and thinking carbon owns half of  the electrons in each bond since carbon  likes to have four valence electrons  carbon is neutral in methane now let's  move on to ammonia nh3  we see that nitrogen likes to make three  bonds when it's neutral and we have  three bonds to hydrogen in the structure  of ammonia  however nitrogen likes to have five  valence electrons and we only have three  coming from bonds this is because  nitrogen contains a lone pair when an  atom has a lone pair you count the lone  pair as belonging just to that atom  itself and so we have five valence  electrons on nitrogen we can look at the  structure of water to explore the  valence of oxygen oxygen is neutral when  it makes two bonds so bonded to these  two hydrogen atoms we have a neutral  molecule of water but its valence is six  this is because oxygen has two lone  pairs so in water oxygen is the owner of  four electrons from lone pairs and half  of the bonds to hydrogen that's six  valence electrons for our fluorine  example we're going to look at  hydrofluoric acid this is a weak acid  but a really toxic molecule fluorine  likes to make one bond when neutral but  it also likes to have seven valence  electrons  so fluorine gets three lone pairs this  gives it six electrons in lone pairs  plus one from the bond to hydrogen this  is seven valence electrons if we  simplify our periodic table even more to  remove the left and the right hand  groups we can come up with a trick to  figure out the formal charge on an  element the number of bonds the element  likes to form when neutral is still  written across the top of a table now if  the element is making one extra bond it  is going to be positively charged and if  it's making one less bond it's going to  be negatively charged let's look at  water once again as our neutral example  water wants to have six valence  electrons and it has four coming from  the lone pair and two coming from bonds  the hydronium ion is a better way to  represent H+ or a proton in an acidic  solution now we have three bonds to  oxygen but we don't want to break the  octet rule we still only want to have  eight total electrons around our central  element so now oxygen has only one lone  pair and a formal positive charge on  oxygen  okay let's count our valence electrons  to see how we figure this out we already  said we have six valence electrons in  water  now with the hydronium ion we have two  electrons coming from lone pairs we also  have three electrons coming from the six  electrons that are shared in bonds  remember oxygen owns half of the  electrons in the bond if we add this up  we see that oxygen has five valence  electrons total well this is one less  negative charge than the six valence  electrons it likes to have when neutral  so if we have one less negative one less  electron oxygen has a formal positive  charge all right so we looked at an  example where oxygen is making three  bonds one extra bond and therefore has  positive charge let's look at an example  where it's making one less bond and has  a formal negative charge  hydroxide is a deprotonated form of  water that exists in basic solutions  let's count electrons we have just one  electron coming from the bond to  hydrogen oxygen is only sharing  electrons in one bond so to complete its  octet we need to add three lone pairs  with the one electron from the shared  bond and the six electrons from lone  pairs we get seven valence electrons on  oxygen this is one more electron than  oxygen likes to have when neutral if we  have one extra negative charge we get a  formal minus charge on oxygen let's  return to our original piece of the  periodic table for a moment that we were  looking at this formal charge trick  works for all of the elements in the  middle three groups so it will work for  nitrogen and phosphorous oxygen and  sulfur and the halogens but it doesn't  work for carbon let's see why Carbon  likes to have four groups around it I'm  going to label these groups are this is  a convention used in organic chemistry  when we don't want to specifically  define what the group is now if we think  about losing our but leaving its  electrons behind on carbon we get an  extra lone pair on carbon  this gives carbon five valence electrons  therefore the atom carries a formal  negative charge now if we think about  carbon ionizing by losing the R group  with a pair of electrons we get a  different story  carbon now has just three valence  electrons it wants to have four so it's  missing one negative charge and it takes  on a formal positive charge itself this  species is called a carbo cation and it  also has three bonds so you can see why  our formal charge trick doesn't quite  work with carbon in this lesson we  looked at the valence of atoms that are  most likely to be bonded to carbon in  organic molecules we looked at some  simple examples and figure  how to determine how many lone pairs are  on each atom we also looked at a trick  for figuring out formal charge on the  groups to the right of carbon and saw  why this trick can't be applied to  carbon itself we'll look at the bonding  of many more elements and practice lewis  structures in another lesson KP here if  you learn something give me a thumbs up  on the way out and for more chemistry  subscribe to my channel
 

